This setup revolves around making EV_battery units from glass_fiber, with MegaSupply feeding the network and Bosch Automotive buying the finished output. At the raw material level, glass_fiber is managed with a min-max style replenishment rule: when stock drops below 4766.0 units, it is brought back up to 14531.31 units. That inventory is reviewed every 21 hours, with a holding cost of $9.163 per unit and a shortage cost of $5.0 per unit. MegaSupply provides the glass_fiber at $133.899 per unit, with lead times varying uniformly between 8.0 hours and 24.0 hours, and the supplier can deliver up to 3208 units per order. Payment to MegaSupply is due exactly 56.0 days after delivery. The inbound move from MegaSupply to the Production Center takes somewhere between 4.0 hours and 13.0 hours. Inside the network, the Production Center handles both glass_fiber and EV_battery, converting 1 unit of glass_fiber into 1 unit of EV_battery through an assembly step that takes exactly 1.0 hour. Finished batteries are then moved from the Production Center to the Warehouse with immediate transfer. On the finished goods side, EV_battery starts with 2790849 units in stock, is reviewed every 1 hour, and carries a holding cost of $8.599 per unit. Demand comes from Bosch Automotive, with orders arriving exponentially with an average of 7.0 hours. Order size is normally centered around 3432450.0 units, varying by about 997855.0 units. Customer shipments have a fixed lead time of 16.0 hours, and the business follows a partial shipment policy with lost remainder, meaning available units are shipped and any shortfall is lost. Each EV_battery sells at $4696.443 per unit, and Bosch Automotive pays exactly 35.0 days after receipt. The modeled network includes MegaSupply, the Production Center, and the Warehouse, and the scenario runs for a horizon of 200 hours.

