This network is built around making laptops, with GlassCore Ltd., NanoMaterials Inc., and Memory Star feeding Main Factory, and finished laptops then moving on to the Storage Facility before serving Dell Technologies. At the material level, the plant uses plastic_pellet, solder_paste, and copper_wire, and it also builds circuit_board units as an internal sub-assembly. Each circuit_board requires 5 units of copper_wire, 3 units of solder_paste, and 2 units of plastic_pellet, while each laptop consumes 4 units of solder_paste, 1 unit of copper_wire, 2 units of plastic_pellet, and 2 units of circuit_board. Plastic_pellet is supplied by GlassCore Ltd. in large periodic lots of 61809.0 units, with scheduled arrivals every 21.0 days; GlassCore’s lead time is exponential with an average of 13.0 days, the purchase cost is $43.133 per unit, payment is due 36.0 days after delivery, and the supplier can provide up to 143657 units per order. That item carries a holding cost of $3.449 per unit and is reviewed every 24 days. Solder_paste comes from NanoMaterials Inc. on an as-needed basis, with a fixed 11.0-day lead time, a unit cost of $348.853, and 58.0-day payment terms. Its inventory is checked every 2 days, with a holding cost of $5.87 per unit and a shortage cost of $21.622 per unit. Copper_wire is sourced from Memory Star in recurring batches of 15193.0 units, and those deliveries arrive somewhere between 16.0 days and 25.0 days; supplier lead time follows an exponential pattern with a mean of 10.0 days, the material costs $32.659 per unit, payment falls between 45.0 days and 75.0 days after receipt, and Memory Star can supply as much as 452661 units per delivery. Copper_wire is reviewed every 19 days and has a shortage penalty of $49.87 per unit. Inside Main Factory, the managed stock includes circuit_board, laptop, solder_paste, plastic_pellet, and copper_wire. The manufacturing step is called molding, and it takes in solder_paste, copper_wire, plastic_pellet, and circuit_board to produce laptop output, with each production cycle varying uniformly between 7.0 days and 22.0 days. On the inventory side, the factory starts with 793725 units of circuit_board on hand, reviewed every 1 day, and shortages there cost $38.957 per unit. Finished laptop stock begins at 1970792 units, is also reviewed every 1 day, and carries a holding cost of $6.553 per unit. Demand comes from Dell Technologies, which places orders for laptop every 35.0 days. Order volume is normally centered around 1354990.0 units with variability of 166601.0 units, customer lead time ranges from 13.0 days to 28.0 days, and if stock is not available the sale is lost rather than backordered. The selling price is $4862.971 per unit, and customer payment is received between 20.0 days and 54.0 days later. The model runs in days over a 730-day horizon, with the first 30 days treated as warm-up, and 10 replications are used.

