Transform the following supply chain scenario into a structured JSON configuration. A schema template follows the description:


This network revolves around three incoming materials—gold_wire, copper_wire, and steel_rod—that feed a Production Center, with finished FPGA and drone units then moving on to a Logistics Center. The bill of materials is fairly layered: each sensor_array is built from 1 unit of gold_wire, 1 unit of steel_rod, and 3 units of copper_wire. From there, each FPGA requires 4 units of steel_rod, 1 unit of gold_wire, 1 unit of copper_wire, and 3 units of sensor_array, while each drone uses 1 unit of sensor_array, 3 units of copper_wire, 1 unit of steel_rod, and 2 units of gold_wire. On the supply side, Process Go. provides gold_wire with supplier lead times ranging from 7.0 weeks to 24.0 weeks, at a cost of $203.501 per unit, with payment due 68.0 days after delivery and a maximum supply of 232349 units per order. Gold_wire itself is replenished on a very long fixed cycle of 66489.0 weeks, and those replenishment arrivals land somewhere between 6.0 weeks and 10.0 weeks. PrecisionParts supplies copper_wire on an as-needed basis, with lead times averaging 16.0 weeks, a unit cost of $200.728, and net 85.0 days payment terms; shipments from PrecisionParts to the Production Center take exactly 6.0 weeks. Copper_wire also carries a holding cost of $6.665 per unit and is reviewed every 15 weeks. ElectroParts handles steel_rod, typically shipping with an average lead time of 17.0 weeks, charging $445.765 per unit, and offering payment terms anywhere from 12.0 days to 72.0 days after receipt, with capacity capped at 724755 units per delivery. Steel_rod is restocked on a variable schedule ranging from 15859.0 weeks to 66970.0 weeks, and each replenishment arrives in a consistent 10.0 weeks; its shortage cost is $24.085 per unit and stock is reviewed every 12 weeks. Inside the operation, the Production Center manages gold_wire, sensor_array, steel_rod, FPGA, and copper_wire in one manufacturing flow and also manages drone, gold_wire, sensor_array, steel_rod, and copper_wire in another. The fabrication step converts steel_rod, gold_wire, copper_wire, and sensor_array into FPGA on a fixed 21.0-week cycle, using the Assembly Line resource. That Assembly Line has capacity for 5 units at a time, runs with a constant service time of 2.061 weeks, and costs $0.35 per week to operate. A separate testing step turns sensor_array, copper_wire, steel_rod, and gold_wire into drone, with cycle times varying uniformly between 2.0 weeks and 5.0 weeks. Inventory starts high in this business: 262678 units of sensor_array, 2610243 units of FPGA, and 2457614 units of drone are already on hand. Sensor_array inventory is reviewed every 1 week, with holding cost at $1.067 per unit and shortage cost at $41.751 per unit. FPGA stock is also reviewed every 1 week, with holding cost of $8.66 per unit and shortage cost of $43.443 per unit, while drone inventory is reviewed every 1 week and carries a shortage cost of $45.145 per unit. Customer demand is substantial. NXP Semiconductors buys FPGA, placing orders somewhere between every 11.0 weeks and 48.0 weeks, with demand volumes ranging from 1373536.0 units to 4103303.0 units per order. FPGA orders are delivered in exactly 6.0 weeks, sold at $1209.229 per unit, and follow a partial backorder approach, meaning available units ship right away and the remainder waits until stock is replenished. Cash collection from NXP Semiconductors comes between 22.0 days and 74.0 days after delivery. Samsung Electronics buys drone on a steadier pattern, ordering every 18.0 weeks for exactly 2319753.0 units, with customer lead time fixed at 25.0 weeks. Drone sales are priced at $471.743 per unit, also with partial backordering, and customer payment arrives between 23.0 days and 44.0 days later. Structurally, the node setup ties Process Go., PrecisionParts, and ElectroParts to the Production Center and the Logistics Center, with FPGA and drone both flowing from Production Center into Logistics Center. The model runs over a 500-week horizon with 10 replications.

