This network centers on Plant A and a Warehouse, with BatteryTech and CarbonSupply Co. feeding the operation and finished goods moving out as drone and CPU products. On the input side, glass_fiber is sourced on an as-needed basis from BatteryTech, with supply taking exactly 17.0 weeks, a purchase cost of $86.499 per unit, and payment due 34.0 days after delivery. That glass_fiber inventory carries a holding cost of $3.101 per unit and is reviewed every 23 weeks. Lithium_cell comes from CarbonSupply Co. with a fixed 29.0-week lead time, a supplier price of $417.633 per unit, and 74.0-day payment terms. Its replenishment follows a min-max style rule: when stock drops below 4126.43 units, it is brought back up to 5796.16 units. Plant A uses these materials in two separate manufacturing flows. In the polishing step, lithium_cell is converted into drone, and each drone requires 3 units of lithium_cell; the cycle time varies uniformly between 7.0 weeks and 20.0 weeks. In the assembly step, glass_fiber is turned into CPU, with 3 units of glass_fiber needed for each CPU, and that process takes a consistent 15.0 weeks. Plant A manages lithium_cell and drone in one production stream, and CPU and glass_fiber in the other, while the Warehouse receives both drone and CPU output from Plant A. Finished goods inventory starts high, at 2059763 units for drone and 820939 units for CPU. Drone stock is reviewed every 1 week and carries a shortage penalty of $17.738 per unit. CPU inventory is also reviewed every 1 week, with a holding cost of $7.305 per unit and a shortage cost of $25.051 per unit. Demand comes from two business customers. HP Enterprise buys drone, placing orders anywhere from every 32.0 weeks to 33.0 weeks, with order sizes ranging from 872320.0 units to 908426.0 units. Customer delivery for drone takes exactly 3.0 weeks, the selling price is $1324.748 per unit, and payment comes in between 10.0 days and 27.0 days; if stock is short, available units are shipped and the rest of the demand is lost. Infineon Technologies purchases CPU, with orders arriving between 25.0 weeks and 38.0 weeks and demand volumes ranging from 1772562.0 units to 4254544.0 units. CPU shipments take between 11.0 weeks and 29.0 weeks to reach the customer, sell at $3242.326 per unit, and are paid on a fixed 43.0-day term. When CPU demand exceeds available inventory, whatever is on hand ships immediately and the remaining balance is backordered. The modeled setup runs for a 100-week horizon with 10 replications.

