hen move into Storage Facility as the warehouse node. Router inventory starts at 812211 units, with a holding cost of $7.65 per unit, a shortage cost of $13.419 per unit, and a 1-week review cycle. Medical_device inventory begins much higher at 4273222 units; its shortage cost is $47.634 per unit and it is also reviewed every 1 week. On the demand side, NXP Semiconductors buys router units. Orders arrive with an exponential pattern averaging 49.0 weeks, and order sizes are approximately 1921341.0 units with variability of 157388.0 units. Customer delivery takes between 15.0 weeks and 22.0 weeks, the selling price is $4508.36 per unit, and payment is received exactly 13.0 days later. If routers are unavailable, the sale is lost. Samsung Electronics purchases medical_device units, with demand arriving on average every 40.0 weeks in fixed lots of 480841.0 units. Delivery to that customer takes 15.0 weeks to 24.0 weeks, the sales price is $496.773 per unit, and cash comes in 41.0 days after delivery. Here, unmet demand is backordered rather than lost. Structurally, the supplier base of GlassCore Ltd., FastChip Corp., and MetalFab Inc. supports the 3 facilities Advanced Fab, Assembly Hub, and Storage Facility, with copper_wire and lithium_cell flowing into Advanced Fab, and finished router and medical_device units flowing onward to storage. The model runs for 200 weeks, with the first 30 weeks treated as warm-up, across 10 replications.

