This network is centered on making routers, with FastChip Corp., CarbonSupply Co., SiliconWorks, MetalFab Inc., and ChipCo feeding the Production Center, and finished routers then moving on to the Warehouse. The bill of materials is fairly layered: sensor_array units are built from 4 units of resin and 4 units of lithium_cell, display_panel units consume 4 units of silicon_wafer, and each motor_assembly uses 2 units of memory_chip. The finished router then pulls together 4 units of display_panel, 3 units of sensor_array, 2 units of lithium_cell, 2 units of silicon_wafer, 2 units of motor_assembly, 2 units of memory_chip, and 1 unit of resin. On the raw material side, silicon_wafer is sourced on demand from two suppliers, with FastChip Corp. delivering on an exponential schedule averaging 10.0 days at $302.908 per unit and payment falling between 25.0 days and 53.0 days after delivery, while ChipCo offers a fixed 15.0-day lead time at $359.877 per unit with 71.0-day payment terms. Lithium_cell is also replenished as needed, coming from CarbonSupply Co. in exactly 17.0 days at $14.151 per unit, with payment due 44.0 days after receipt and a maximum order size of 451076 units; its shortage cost is $43.886 per unit and stock is reviewed every 9 days. Resin is handled more traditionally, with orders triggered when inventory drops below 3991.76 units and raised back up to 8669.55 units. SiliconWorks supplies it in a reliable 6.0 days at $409.697 per unit, allows 85.0 days to pay, and can deliver up to 533744 units per order; the lane from SiliconWorks to the Production Center adds a further 4.0 days of transfer time. Resin also carries a holding cost of $1.046 per unit, a shortage penalty of $16.86 per unit, and a 29-day review cycle. Memory_chip is another demand-driven item, bought from MetalFab Inc. at $473.342 per unit with lead time varying uniformly between 15.0 days and 29.0 days, 37.0-day payment terms, and transportation to the plant taking between 3.0 days and 7.0 days. Keeping memory_chip on hand costs $6.581 per unit, shortages cost $49.687 per unit, and inventory is checked every 24 days. Inside the operation, the Production Center manages display_panel, sensor_array, memory_chip, silicon_wafer, lithium_cell, resin, router, and motor_assembly inventory. It starts with 4973640 units of sensor_array, 3509990 units of display_panel, 3417439 units of motor_assembly, and 2663410 units of router already in stock. The in-process inventories are watched closely: sensor_array has a shortage cost of $36.568 per unit with review every 1 day, display_panel has a shortage cost of $27.49 per unit with the same 1-day review, and motor_assembly carries a holding cost of $6.133 per unit and is likewise reviewed every 1 day. Actual router production happens at the Production Center through a cutting step that combines display_panel, sensor_array, lithium_cell, silicon_wafer, motor_assembly, memory_chip, and resin into router output, and each run takes a fixed 19.0 days. That step depends on the Welding Station, which has capacity for 3 units, a constant service time of 1.725 days, and batching turned on at 43 units with a maximum wait of 11.529 days. The station is not perfectly reliable either: uptime is exponentially distributed with an average of 118.825 days, downtime is a fixed 20.321 days, and it costs $15.453 per day to operate. On the demand side, ABB Industrial buys the router. Orders arrive every 14.0 days, with demand centered around 1784075.0 units and varying by about 40368.0 units. Customer lead time runs between 4.0 days and 10.0 days. If stock is short, available units are shipped and the rest of the demand is lost. The router sells at $41.825 per unit, and ABB Industrial pays between 28.0 days and 49.0 days after delivery. The model runs for 365 days total, with the first 30 days excluded as warm-up, and uses 10 replications.

