This network revolves around two incoming materials, copper_wire and glass_fiber, which feed a Processing Unit and then a Storage Facility before finished goods move out to customers. PolymerSource supplies copper_wire to the Processing Unit with lead times varying between 9.0 weeks and 15.0 weeks, at a purchase cost of $457.736 per unit, and the supplier is paid exactly 81.0 days after delivery. Copper wire is replenished in periodic lots ranging from 4180.0 units to 42215.0 units, with scheduled arrivals every 15.0 weeks; its carrying cost is $2.062 per unit, the shortage penalty is $1.959 per unit, and stock is reviewed every 11 weeks. CarbonSupply Co. sends glass_fiber to the same Processing Unit, reliably in 4.0 weeks, at $305.207 per unit, with payment terms ranging from 47.0 days to 88.0 days. For glass_fiber, the business uses a min-max style trigger: when inventory drops below 2695.32 units, it is restored up to 9052.75 units. Holding this material costs $1.691 per unit, shortages are expensive at $34.587 per unit, and review happens every 30 weeks. Inside the operation, a cooling_unit is built as a sub-assembly using 2 units of glass_fiber and 4 units of copper_wire, and there are 3885855 units of cooling_unit already on hand; its shortage cost is $18.47 per unit with a 1 week review cadence. The SoC line at the Processing Unit runs an inspection step that consumes 3 units of glass_fiber and 3 units of copper_wire to produce 1 SoC, and that cycle takes anywhere from 1.0 week to 18.0 weeks. Finished SoC inventory starts at 1117491 units, with a shortage cost of $18.433 per unit and reviews every 1 week. Drone fabrication also takes place at the Processing Unit, using 1 unit of cooling_unit plus 3 units of glass_fiber and 3 units of copper_wire to make 1 drone, with processing time ranging from 5.0 weeks to 19.0 weeks. Drone stock begins at 4101556 units, with a holding cost of $8.564 per unit, a shortage cost of $38.097 per unit, and inventory checks every 1 week. The site also runs an Assembly Line with capacity for 3 units, a fixed service time of 6.645 weeks, operating cost of $19.778 per week, and a failure pattern of 239.167 weeks of uptime followed by 12.19 weeks of downtime. On the demand side, Apple Supply buys SoC, placing orders every 39.0 weeks with demand centered around 3487224.0 units and varying by about 531966.0 units. Shipments to Apple Supply take 7.0 weeks, any shortfall is backordered, the selling price is $1501.962 per unit, and payment is received 81.0 days later. TechCorp purchases drone, ordering every 29.0 weeks for a fixed 1830875.0 units, with delivery taking 21.0 weeks. For that account, available stock is shipped and the remainder is lost, and the drone sells at $2005.59 per unit with customer payment arriving between 35.0 days and 54.0 days. In terms of physical flow, PolymerSource and CarbonSupply Co. feed the Processing Unit, and both SoC and drone move from the Processing Unit into the Storage Facility. The model runs for a 100 week horizon, with results collected after a 30 week warm-up period.

