and daily review every 1 day. Final assembly is also concentrated at the Pilot Line. The cutting step converts microprocessor and battery_pack into CPU in a fixed 10.0-day cycle, and each CPU requires 2 units of microprocessor plus 4 units of battery_pack. This step depends on the Quality Scanner, which has capacity for 4 units, a service time varying uniformly between 2.709 days and 9.238 days, and an operating cost of $18.162 per day. That scanner runs for exactly 229.659 days before failure and stays down for exactly 48.892 days when it breaks. CPU inventory starts at 4938611 units, is reviewed every 1 day, and carries a holding cost of $6.805 per unit. The polishing step at the same Pilot Line produces medical_device from motor_assembly and steel_rod in exactly 12.0 days, with each medical_device needing 2 units of motor_assembly and 1 unit of steel_rod. Medical_device inventory opens at 4356879 units, is checked every 1 day, and incurs a shortage cost of $5.294 per unit. The assembly step makes smartphone from frame_structure in a consistent 10.0-day cycle, using 2 units of frame_structure for each smartphone. Smartphone stock begins at 4583187 units, is reviewed every 1 day, and costs $4.129 per unit to hold. Supporting this flow is a CNC Machine with capacity of 2 units, an exponentially distributed service time with a mean of 8.001 days, and an operating cost of $10.367 per day. Finished goods—CPU, medical_device, and smartphone—move from the Pilot Line to the Regional Depot. On the demand side, Schneider Electric buys CPU, with orders arriving on average every 49.0 days and demand centered around 2536587.0 units, varying by about 452669.0 units. Customers are promised shipment in exactly 4.0 days, the selling price is $4518.46 per unit, and payments come in between 35.0 days and 47.0 days. If CPU stock is short, available units ship immediately and the remainder is backordered. Infineon Technologies purchases medical_device, with demand arrivals averaging 16.0 days and order size fixed at 4113736.0 units. Delivery to that customer takes exactly 12.0 days, the product sells at $2751.247 per unit, and payment is received between 45.0 days and 46.0 days. Here the policy is full backorder, so customers wait until stock is replenished. ABB Industrial orders smartphone, with requests arriving on average every 34.0 days and quantities ranging from 297824.0 units to 3944603.0 units. Lead time to the customer is exactly 28.0 days, the sale price is $1215.622 per unit, and payment arrives in 46.0 days to 88.0 days. If inventory is tight, the company ships what it has and backorders the balance. Overall, the network links CarbonSupply Co. and GlobalComponents as upstream partners to the Pilot Line and Regional Depot, combining high starting inventories with long-cycle industrial demand and a mix of daily review discipline, supplier payment lags, and resource-based production constraints.

