essor, copper_wire, and steel_rod into router output. A second manufacturing flow at the same Main Factory handles microprocessor, frame_structure, medical_device, and steel_rod through testing, with a fixed 12.0-hour cycle that turns steel_rod, frame_structure, and microprocessor into medical_device. Inventory is already on hand in substantial volumes, with 755579 units of frame_structure in process, 325748 units of router finished stock, and 535363 units of medical_device ready or available in inventory. The frame_structure stock has a holding cost of $9.211 per unit, a shortage cost of $46.133 per unit, and is checked every 1 hour. Router inventory is also reviewed every 1 hour and carries a shortage penalty of $8.888 per unit. Distribution-wise, finished router and medical_device output both move from Main Factory to Regional Depot, while the supplier side of the network links ChipCo, MegaSupply, and CarbonSupply Co. into Main Factory, and the operating footprint explicitly includes Main Factory and Regional Depot. On the demand side, Sony Electronics buys router, placing orders at intervals between 34.0 and 59.0 hours, with requested volumes ranging from 1581044.0 units to 2535949.0 units. Customer delivery for Sony takes between 3.0 and 5.0 hours, the selling price is $884.722 per unit, and payment comes in between 43.0 and 60.0 days. If router stock runs short, available units are shipped and the rest of the demand is lost. Samsung Electronics, by contrast, purchases medical_device every 15.0 hours flat, with demand fixed at 1782856.0 units each time. Delivery to Samsung takes between 6.0 and 11.0 hours, the sales price is $1040.864 per unit, and the account is paid exactly 66.0 days later; any shortfall is backordered rather than lost. The scenario is modeled over a 500-hour horizon with 5 replications.

