This network revolves around Advanced Fab, supported by a Fulfillment Hub, with inbound supply coming from ElectroParts, GlassCore Ltd., NanoMaterials Inc., FastChip Corp., and PolymerSource. The plant works with two basic inputs, lithium_cell and solder_paste, and turns them into several built-up components before shipping finished goods. In the bill of materials, each battery_pack uses 2 units of solder_paste, each cooling_unit uses 2 units of lithium_cell, and each power_module uses 3 units of solder_paste. On the finished side, a tablet requires 1 unit of lithium_cell and 1 unit of solder_paste, a smartphone is assembled from 2 units of battery_pack, 2 units of power_module, and 3 units of cooling_unit, and an FPGA uses 2 units of cooling_unit plus 2 units of battery_pack. Lithium_cell is sourced on an as-needed basis, with inventory reviewed every 8 hours and a holding charge of $9.403 per unit. Solder_paste, by contrast, is replenished on a regular cycle, with batch quantities averaging 44328.0 units and varying by about 11207.0 units, and deliveries arriving somewhere between 12.0 hours and 24.0 hours. Starting stocks are substantial: 2184390 units of battery_pack, 400509 units of cooling_unit, 2449184 units of power_module, 3434938 units of tablet, 261475 units of smartphone, and 1350875 units of FPGA. Battery_pack is reviewed every 1 hour and carries a shortage penalty of $1.452 per unit, cooling_unit is also reviewed every 1 hour with a much steeper shortage cost of $20.619 per unit, tablet inventory is checked every 1 hour with holding cost of $6.5 per unit and shortage cost of $7.725 per unit, smartphone is reviewed every 1 hour with holding cost of $5.656 per unit and shortage cost of $46.165 per unit, and FPGA is reviewed every 1 hour with a shortage cost of $8.476 per unit. Supplier terms are quite mixed. ElectroParts provides lithium_cell with a fixed 17.0-hour lead time at $116.651 per unit, payment due 10.0 days after delivery, and can supply up to 525852 units per order; shipments from ElectroParts to Advanced Fab take between 3.0 hours and 4.0 hours. NanoMaterials Inc. is another lithium_cell source, with lead time ranging from 2.0 hours to 22.0 hours, a unit cost of $499.346, payment terms between 13.0 days and 19.0 days, capacity of 525313 units, and transfer time to Advanced Fab between 4.0 hours and 6.0 hours. PolymerSource also sells lithium_cell, with an exponential lead time averaging 6.0 hours, a very low cost of $8.164 per unit, net 89.0 days payment, and a maximum order size of 72607 units. For solder_paste, GlassCore Ltd. delivers in 14.0 to 27.0 hours at $417.223 per unit, with 9.0-day payment terms and capacity capped at 826522 units, while FastChip Corp. supplies with an exponential lead time averaging 8.0 hours at $459.185 per unit and payment due between 15.0 days and 23.0 days. At Advanced Fab, tablet production is handled through a molding step that consumes lithium_cell and solder_paste and produces tablet output in a 7.0-hour to 24.0-hour cycle, using the Laser Cutter. That Laser Cutter has capacity for 5 units at a time, works with an exponential service time averaging 4.65 hours, and costs $6.586 per hour to run. Smartphone output is built in an assembly operation using battery_pack, power_module, and cooling_unit, with each cycle taking between 16.0 hours and 17.0 hours. FPGA production runs through a cutting step using cooling_unit and battery_pack, and that cycle ranges from 5.0 hours to 10.0 hours. Another shared asset in the system is the Quality Scanner, which has capacity for 3 units, a fixed 4.82-hour service time, and batch processing turned on at 47 units with a maximum wait of 14.812 hours. It is also failure-prone: uptime is exponentially distributed with a mean of 123.117 hours, downtime is a constant 21.849 hours, and operating cost is $14.959 per hour. Finished tablets, smartphones, and FPGA units move from Advanced Fab to the Fulfillment Hub for outbound fulfillment. On