ed every 1 hour and costs $3.221 per unit to hold. At the Assembly Hub, the fabrication process turns microprocessor, memory_chip, and lithium_cell into SoC, and that work takes exactly 24.0 hours per batch. The Assembly Hub manages lithium_cell, microprocessor, memory_chip, and SoC inventory, beginning with 1577428 units of SoC in stock. Finished goods from both plants move to the Distribution Center, with router flowing from the Manufacturing Site and SoC coming from the Assembly Hub. On the demand side, Sony Electronics buys router, placing orders somewhere between every 35.0 hours and 36.0 hours, with order sizes ranging from 378633.0 units to 862344.0 units. Customer delivery for router takes 25.0 hours, the selling price is $32.82 per unit, and payment is collected 19.0 days later; if demand exceeds available stock, available units are shipped and the rest is lost. Ross Associates purchases SoC, ordering every 27.0 hours to 52.0 hours, with demand fixed at 3292899.0 units each time. SoC orders are delivered in 6.0 hours, sold at $4481.386 per unit, and paid 36.0 days after delivery, again with partial fulfillment and any unfilled balance lost. Overall, the modeled system includes MetalFab Inc., Memory Star, GlobalComponents, the Manufacturing Site, the Assembly Hub, and the Distribution Center, and it is observed over a 500-hour horizon with 10 replications.

