to industrial_sensor in a cycle ranging from 8.0 weeks to 15.0 weeks. The Assembly Hub manages lithium_cell, microprocessor, carbon_fiber, and medical_device stock and performs testing to turn microprocessor, carbon_fiber, and lithium_cell into medical_device, with each cycle taking between 6.0 weeks and 10.0 weeks. A shared Robot Arm supports the operation with capacity for 3 units at a time, a fixed service time of 8.959 weeks, uptime of 176.261 weeks, downtime of 19.752 weeks, and an operating cost of $6.034 per week. Finished goods then move into the Storage Facility: industrial_sensor from the Pilot Line and medical_device from the Assembly Hub. Starting inventory is substantial, at 3159049 units of industrial_sensor and 866302 units of medical_device. Holding the industrial_sensor costs $6.001 per unit and that stock is reviewed every 1 week. For medical_device, holding cost is $2.318 per unit, shortage cost is $24.944 per unit, and review also happens every 1 week. On the demand side, Sony Electronics buys industrial_sensor with order arrivals following an exponential pattern averaging 31.0 weeks, each order fixed at 4250779.0 units. Customers are promised delivery in exactly 13.0 weeks, any shortfall is backordered, the selling price is $2779.796 per unit, and Sony pays 78.0 days after delivery. ABB Industrial purchases medical_device every 46.0 weeks, with order sizes varying from 1120878.0 units to 3582117.0 units. Their delivery lead time runs between 6.0 weeks and 26.0 weeks, unmet demand is also backordered, the selling price is $36.754 per unit, and payment arrives after 30.0 days. The model runs over a 365-week horizon, with results collected after a 30-week warm-up period.

