This network revolves around three incoming materials—aluminum_sheet, lithium_cell, and carbon_fiber—and turns them into two sellable items, SoC and tablet, with a motor_assembly sub-assembly feeding part of the tablet build. The motor_assembly itself consumes 2 units of aluminum_sheet, 1 unit of carbon_fiber, and 5 units of lithium_cell. On the finished goods side, each SoC uses 3 units of lithium_cell and 2 units of carbon_fiber, while each tablet requires 2 units of motor_assembly, 4 units of aluminum_sheet, and 1 unit of lithium_cell. Sourcing is split across three vendors in the network: BatteryTech supplies aluminum_sheet at $427.779 per unit with a fixed 12.0 hours supplier lead time and payment due 74.0 days after delivery, MegaSupply provides lithium_cell at $214.6 per unit with lead times ranging from 17.0 to 28.0 hours, net 60.0 days, and a maximum order size of 476057 units, and FastChip Corp. supplies carbon_fiber at $82.545 per unit with an exponential lead time averaging 7.0 hours and payment due 14.0 days after receipt. Aluminum_sheet is sourced on demand, and shipments from BatteryTech to Plant A take between 6.0 and 11.0 hours. Lithium_cell is controlled with a min-max style replenishment trigger: when stock drops below 909.28 units, it is brought back up to 1227.86 units; it carries a holding cost of $3.286 per unit, a shortage penalty of $37.062 per unit, and is reviewed every 19 hours. Carbon_fiber comes in on a regular schedule every 15.0 hours, in quantities centered around 68655.0 units with variability of 12590.0 units, and it has a shortage cost of $35.961 per unit with review every 19 hours; transfers from FastChip Corp. to Plant A take exactly 11.0 hours. Plant A manages lithium_cell, SoC, and carbon_fiber inventory and runs a molding step that converts lithium_cell and carbon_fiber into SoC in 2.0 to 4.0 hours per cycle. The broader operation also has an Injection Molder resource with capacity for 5 units, an exponential service time averaging 2.442 hours, and an operating cost of $16.494 per hour. Integration Center manages lithium_cell, aluminum_sheet, motor_assembly, and tablet inventory and performs fabrication, turning motor_assembly, aluminum_sheet, and lithium_cell into tablet on a fixed 24.0-hour cycle. Regional Depot acts as the warehouse receiving SoC from Plant A and tablet from Integration Center. Starting stocks are quite large: 1412156 units of motor_assembly, 1971847 units of SoC, and 640978 units of tablet. Inventory economics differ by item, too: motor_assembly has a shortage cost of $21.292 per unit with review every 1 hour, SoC carries a shortage cost of $20.202 per unit with review every 1 hour, and tablet has a holding cost of $6.487 per unit with review every 1 hour. On the demand side, Ross Associates buys SoC, placing orders every 12.0 hours with demand roughly 1712194.0 units on average and variability of 430642.0 units; promised delivery runs between 16.0 and 22.0 hours, the selling price is $1542.671 per unit, customer payment comes between 10.0 and 42.0 days, and shortages are handled by shipping available units immediately and backordering the rest. Dell Technologies purchases tablet with order arrivals anywhere from 32.0 to 40.0 hours apart and demand between 2615964.0 and 2921298.0 units; delivery to the customer is a consistent 24.0 hours, the selling price is $461.982 per unit, payment is received in 9.0 to 77.0 days, and any unfilled demand is fully backordered. The modeled network explicitly links BatteryTech, MegaSupply, and FastChip Corp. to Plant A, and connects Plant A, Integration Center, and Regional Depot as the operating facilities. The scenario runs in hours over a 100-hour horizon, with results collected after a 50-hour warm-up period.

