This network centers on a Manufacturing Site feeding a Distribution Center, with four suppliers upstream and three major customers downstream over a 500-week planning horizon, evaluated across 5 replications. The raw inputs are lithium_cell, ceramic_substrate, epoxy_compound, and carbon_fiber. Those materials are turned first into several pre-assembled parts: each frame_structure uses 5 units of lithium_cell and 3 units of carbon_fiber, each cooling_unit consumes 4 units of epoxy_compound, 1 unit of lithium_cell, and 5 units of carbon_fiber, and each display_panel requires 5 units of ceramic_substrate. Final assembly then branches into three products: each SoC needs 3 units of epoxy_compound, 2 units of carbon_fiber, 3 units of ceramic_substrate, and 4 units of frame_structure; each drone uses 4 units of cooling_unit, 4 units of display_panel, and 2 units of frame_structure; and each laptop is built from 1 unit of frame_structure plus 3 units of lithium_cell. On the supply side, SiliconWorks provides lithium_cell on a demand-driven basis, with lead times averaging 16.0 weeks, a purchase cost of $352.028 per unit, payment due between 52.0 weeks and 70.0 weeks after delivery, a maximum order size of 170064 units, and an 11.0-week transfer to the plant. Lithium_cell shortages are expensive at $10.459 per unit, and that stock position is reviewed every 30 weeks. GlobalComponents supplies ceramic_substrate with lead times averaging 29.0 weeks at $69.181 per unit, on payment terms of 28.0 to 56.0 weeks, while the plant receives periodic replenishment in batches every 1841.0 weeks, arriving every 19.0 weeks, plus a 2.0-week transfer into the Manufacturing Site. Ceramic_substrate carries a holding cost of $5.891 per unit, a shortage cost of $25.244 per unit, and a 25-week review cycle. CarbonSupply Co. handles epoxy_compound as a demand-driven source, shipping in 10.0 to 13.0 weeks, charging $228.772 per unit, with a fixed 17.0-week payment term and capacity capped at 273303 units. MegaSupply provides carbon_fiber with supplier lead times averaging 20.0 weeks at $97.087 per unit and net 71.0-week payment, while internal replenishment is scheduled around 15732.0 weeks on average with variability of 3555.0 weeks and arrives on a consistent 20.0-week cycle. Carbon_fiber inventory costs run at $7.268 per unit held, $46.622 per unit short, and reviews happen every 28 weeks. Inside the plant, frame_structure starts with 3204523 units on hand and carries a holding cost of $9.886 per unit with reviews every 1 week. Cooling_unit begins at 2159053 units. Display_panel opens at 798319 units, with holding cost at $9.973 per unit and a 1-week review cadence. Finished goods inventory is also substantial: SoC starts at 406030 units, with holding cost of $2.263 per unit, shortage cost of $39.3 per unit, and review every 1 week; drone begins at 927178 units; and laptop starts at 2556353 units, with holding cost of $2.91 per unit, shortage cost of $33.312 per unit, and a 1-week review. Production is handled at the Manufacturing Site through three different flows. The SoC line runs an assembly step using epoxy_compound, carbon_fiber, ceramic_substrate, and frame_structure, and every job takes exactly 17.0 weeks. That step requires the Injection Molder, which has capacity for 4 units, works with service times averaging 1.605 weeks, and processes material in batches of 18 units with a maximum wait of 11.318 weeks before release. The machine is not perfectly reliable: uptime averages 359.178 weeks and downtime averages 14.122 weeks. Operating that resource costs $18.207 per week. Drone output comes from a testing stage that consumes cooling_unit, display_panel, and frame_structure, with processing time ranging from 10.0 weeks to 23.0 weeks. Laptop production uses a molding step that combines frame_structure and lithium_cell, and in practice it takes 10.0 weeks flat, since the range is 10.0 weeks to 10.0 weeks. Outbound finished goods move from the Manufacturing Site to the Distribution Center for SoC, drone, and laptop. On the demand side, Broadcom Limited orders SoC every 33.0 weeks, with demand centered around 2086763.0 units and varying by about 240095.0 units. Customers expect delivery in 15.0 to 17.0 weeks, the selling price is $4388.649 per unit, payment is received 83.0 weeks later, and if product is unavailable the sale is lost. Lenovo Group buys drone every 38.0 weeks in fixed orders of 2705799.0 units, with a 6.0-week customer lead time, a selling price of $4280.496 per unit, and payment arriving 24.0 weeks after delivery; this business also follows a lost-sales approach. Apple Supply purchases laptop every 56.0 weeks with steady demand of 2106224.0 units, expects shipment in 9.0 weeks, pays $2375.329 per unit, and settles invoices in 33.0 weeks; in this case unmet demand is backordered rather than lost. Overall, the node structure links SiliconWorks, GlobalComponents, CarbonSupply Co., and MegaSupply into the Manufacturing Site and then onward to the Distribution Center, creating a multi-product operation with long-cycle inbound programs, very large inventory positions, and customer volumes measured in the millions of units per week-based planning cycle.

