This network revolves around Advanced Fab, which takes in copper_wire and epoxy_compound, builds heat_sink sub-components, and ultimately supplies tablet and GPU products through the Fulfillment Hub over a 365-week planning horizon with 1 replication. The heat_sink uses 1 unit of epoxy_compound and 1 unit of copper_wire. Each tablet then requires 1 unit of epoxy_compound, 2 units of heat_sink, and 4 units of copper_wire, while each GPU consumes 3 units of copper_wire, 1 unit of heat_sink, and 2 units of epoxy_compound. Copper_wire is sourced from MetalFab Inc. under a min-max replenishment setup: when stock drops below 1946.48 units, it is brought back up to 5060.0 units. That supplier typically delivers with an exponential lead time averaging 6.0 weeks, charges $491.716 per unit, allows payment 21.0 days after delivery, and can supply up to 909495 units per order. Copper_wire carrying cost is $4.731 per unit, with inventory reviewed every 5 weeks. Epoxy_compound comes from NanoMaterials Inc. in scheduled deliveries, with shipment quantities varying uniformly between 56112.0 units and 70059.0 units, arriving exactly every 24.0 weeks. NanoMaterials Inc. has an exponential lead time with a mean of 19.0 weeks, charges $313.049 per unit, offers 57.0-day payment terms, and is limited to 185374 units per delivery. Epoxy_compound holding cost is $0.756 per unit, and that stock is reviewed every 20 weeks. Inside the operation, Advanced Fab manages heat_sink, copper_wire, tablet, and epoxy_compound in one manufacturing flow and heat_sink, GPU, epoxy_compound, and copper_wire in another, while the Fulfillment Hub serves as the warehouse node. The tablet line is described as a testing step at Advanced Fab, using epoxy_compound, heat_sink, and copper_wire to produce tablet on a fixed 3.0-week cycle. The GPU line is an assembly step at the same site, consuming copper_wire, heat_sink, and epoxy_compound to turn out GPU every 5.0 weeks. On the shop floor there is a Laser Cutter with capacity for 5 units, an exponential service time averaging 7.471 weeks, batching enabled at 25 units, a maximum wait of 14.41 weeks before a batch is released, and an operating cost of $5.258 per week. Starting inventory is quite high: 2417298 units of heat_sink, 4922505 units of tablet, and 4853616 units of GPU. Heat_sink is reviewed every 1 week and carries a shortage penalty of $19.89 per unit. Tablet inventory is also checked every 1 week, with a holding cost of $9.834 per unit. GPU stock is reviewed every 1 week as well, with a holding cost of $2.577 per unit and a shortage cost of $31.129 per unit. On the demand side, Apple Supply buys tablet, placing orders every 40.0 weeks flat, with order size ranging uniformly from 605726.0 units to 3458411.0 units. Customer lead time for those tablet orders runs between 1.0 week and 8.0 weeks, the selling price is $1438.052 per unit, and payment is received 45.0 days after delivery. If tablet inventory is short, available units are shipped and the rest of the demand is lost. Bosch Automotive purchases GPU, with order arrivals following an exponential pattern averaging 30.0 weeks. Its demand ranges from 2262446.0 units to 2967789.0 units, delivery to the customer takes exactly 1.0 week, the selling price is $1742.889 per unit, and cash collection comes between 15.0 days and 26.0 days after shipment. The same partial-fulfillment, lost-remainder rule applies there too. Structurally, the network links MetalFab Inc. and NanoMaterials Inc. to Advanced Fab, and then sends both tablet and GPU from Advanced Fab to the Fulfillment Hub.

