This network makes two electronics products, SoC units and routers, using solder_paste, copper_wire, epoxy_compound, steel_rod, and a built-up part called circuit_board. The circuit_board is assembled from 3 units of copper_wire, 4 units of steel_rod, 2 units of epoxy_compound, and 4 units of solder_paste. The SoC itself consumes 3 units of copper_wire, 3 units of solder_paste, and 2 units of epoxy_compound, while each router requires 1 unit of circuit_board plus 3 units of steel_rod. All 8 suppliers feed into the Processing Unit, and finished SoC and router output is then sent on to the Warehouse, with the network centered on the Processing Unit and the Warehouse as the 2 facility locations in the operating footprint. Raw material purchasing is largely triggered as needed by demand. For solder_paste, PolymerSource delivers at a fixed 8.0 weeks and charges $222.337 per unit, with payment falling between 25.0 weeks and 84.0 weeks after receipt; CarbonSupply Co. is the faster and cheaper alternate source, with an exponential lead time averaging 3.0 weeks, a unit cost of $59.716, 62.0-week payment terms, and a maximum order size of 132964 units, plus a 3.0-week transfer to the Processing Unit. Copper_wire can come from NanoMaterials Inc. with an exponential lead time averaging 4.0 weeks at $166.575 per unit and payment between 8.0 weeks and 35.0 weeks, or from SiliconWorks with a consistent 27.0-week lead time, $118.195 per unit cost, and payment due between 32.0 weeks and 52.0 weeks. Epoxy_compound is sourced from MegaSupply in exactly 18.0 weeks at $211.727 per unit with net 74.0 weeks and a further 4.0-week transfer, from Memory Star in a 8.0 to 10.0 week range at $57.07 per unit with payment between 43.0 weeks and 62.0 weeks and capacity capped at 145393 units, or from BatteryTech with an exponential lead time with a mean of 7.0 weeks at $19.207 per unit and payment due between 10.0 weeks and 22.0 weeks. Steel_rod is supplied by Process Go. in anywhere from 6.0 weeks to 10.0 weeks at $298.127 per unit, with 52.0-week payment terms and a maximum order size of 352157 units. Inventory settings vary by item: solder_paste is reviewed every 2 weeks with a holding charge of $1.757 per unit and a shortage penalty of $4.425 per unit; epoxy_compound is reviewed every 9 weeks with $7.168 per unit in holding cost and $43.191 per unit in shortage cost; steel_rod is checked every 16 weeks with holding at $3.207 per unit and shortage at $17.225 per unit. The fabricated circuit_board starts with 2449339 units on hand, is reviewed every 1 week, carries $4.854 per unit in holding cost, and incurs $26.956 per unit when short. Finished goods inventory is also substantial: SoC begins with 3240969 units and has a holding cost of $2.239 per unit with a 1-week review cadence, while router opens at 747283 units, is reviewed every 1 week, and carries $9.839 per unit in holding cost plus a shortage cost of $48.911 per unit. Production happens in the Processing Unit through 2 separate manufacturing steps. The bonding step converts copper_wire, solder_paste, and epoxy_compound into SoC, and its cycle time ranges from 14.0 weeks to 26.0 weeks. This step depends on a Robot Arm resource with capacity for 3 units, a service time between 3.351 weeks and 8.622 weeks, and an operating expense of $15.233 per week. The Robot Arm is not perfectly reliable either: it runs for an average of 56.96 weeks before a failure and then stays down for an average of 18.784 weeks. The second step, fabrication, turns circuit_board and steel_rod into router units in anywhere from 1.0 week to 21.0 weeks, with no named resource requirement attached. On the demand side, Broadcom Limited buys SoC, placing orders every 17.0 to 23.0 weeks for between 1277999.0 units and 3657113.0 units at a selling price of $4448.101 per unit. Customer deliveries for Broadcom take exactly 3.0 weeks, and the business follows a partial backorder approach, meaning available units ship immediately and the remainder is backordered; payment arrives between 44.0 weeks and 77.0 weeks later. Router demand is split across Samsung Electronics and Sony Electronics. Samsung Electronics orders routers with an exponential interarrival time averaging 10.0 weeks, always for 3133445.0 units, at $255.528 per unit, with a 28.0-week customer lead time and a full backorder policy; payment comes between 26.0 weeks and 42.0 weeks. Sony Electronics places router orders every 24.0 weeks flat, with demand centered around 123716.0 units and varying by about 24816.0 units, pays $3218.856 per unit, and receives shipments in 11.0 weeks; any unmet volume is also backordered, and payment is made 55.0 weeks after delivery. The model runs in weeks over a 500-week horizon, with 10 replications.

