This network makes two electronics products, tablet and CPU, using four incoming materials: plastic_pellet, glass_fiber, gold_wire, and lithium_cell. Upstream, ElectroParts feeds plastic_pellet to the Production Center with a reliable 2.0 hours lead time at $203.13 per unit, and invoices are settled 54.0 days after delivery. FastChip Corp. also supplies glass_fiber into the Production Center, with lead time varying between 13.0 hours and 26.0 hours, a purchase cost of $1.895 per unit, and payment due between 25.0 days and 42.0 days. A second glass_fiber source, PrecisionParts, serves the same Production Center with a 9.0 to 16.0 hours lead time, a much higher cost of $257.443 per unit, 53.0 to 76.0 days payment terms, and a maximum order size of 129906 units. Gold_wire comes from Memory Star with an exponential lead time averaging 14.0 hours, a unit cost of $393.422, payment due between 15.0 days and 68.0 days, and supply capped at 350806 units per order. Lithium_cell is purchased from GlobalComponents at $105.402 per unit, with lead time averaging 25.0 hours, payment due between 16.0 days and 62.0 days, and capacity limited to 277139 units per delivery. On the replenishment side, plastic_pellet follows a min-max style policy: when stock drops below 2967.66 units, it is brought back up to 5543.19 units. Glass_fiber and gold_wire are both sourced on demand; glass_fiber carries a holding cost of $2.111 per unit, a shortage penalty of $5.614 per unit, and is reviewed every 3 hours, while gold_wire has a holding cost of $0.258 per unit and is reviewed every 1 hour. Lithium_cell arrives in regular lots of 54483.0 units, with deliveries scheduled somewhere between 17.0 hours and 18.0 hours apart, and it incurs a holding cost of $6.152 per unit with inventory checks every 13 hours. Internally, several fabricated components are built into the final mix. A circuit_board uses 4 units of plastic_pellet, 1 unit of lithium_cell, 4 units of glass_fiber, and 2 units of gold_wire, and starts with 1392043 units on hand; it is reviewed every 1 hour and faces a shortage cost of $2.777 per unit. A lens_assembly consumes 1 unit of gold_wire and 4 units of lithium_cell, with an initial stock of 4663494 units. A heat_sink requires 1 unit of glass_fiber and 2 units of lithium_cell, begins at 735821 units, and carries a holding cost of $0.915 per unit, a shortage cost of $40.08 per unit, and a 1 hour review cycle. Final assembly is split across two manufacturing sites. At the Production Center, which manages gold_wire, tablet, lens_assembly, glass_fiber, and plastic_pellet, the testing step converts lens_assembly, glass_fiber, plastic_pellet, and gold_wire into tablet, and each run takes exactly 18.0 hours. That work uses the Assembly Line, which has capacity for 1 unit at a time, a service time averaging 1.215 hours, and batching turned on for 71 units with a maximum wait of 3.76 hours. The line is not perfectly reliable either: it stays up for a consistent 414.744 hours, then goes down for 48.129 hours, and costs $7.863 per hour to operate. On the product recipe itself, each tablet needs 1 unit of lens_assembly, 1 unit of glass_fiber, 2 units of plastic_pellet, and 1 unit of gold_wire. The Pilot Line handles the coating step for CPU production, managing circuit_board, heat_sink, lithium_cell, CPU, and glass_fiber. Each CPU run takes 4.0 hours and combines 2 units of circuit_board, 1 unit of heat_sink, 3 units of lithium_cell, and 4 units of glass_fiber. Downstream flow is simple: tablet moves from the Production Center to the Logistics Center, and CPU moves from the Pilot Line to the Logistics Center. Starting finished goods inventory is 2706991 units for tablet and 3618987 units for CPU. Tablet inventory has a holding cost of $9.442 per unit, a shortage cost of $11.339 per unit, and is reviewed every 1 hour. Customer demand comes from Broadcom Limited for tablet and Schneider Electric for CPU. Broadcom Limited places orders every 44.0 hours for 996473.0 units, expects delivery in 21.0 hours, accepts partial shipments with the remainder backordered, pays $4397.783 per unit, and settles invoices 53.0 days later. Schneider Electric orders CPU every 46.0 hours, with demand centered around 2926215.0 units and varying by about 355811.0 units, expects delivery in 2.0 to 9.0 hours, and if stock is unavailable the sale is lost. CPU sells at $3679.929 per unit and customer payment arrives 63.0 days after shipment. The full network therefore links 5 suppliers, 3 facilities, and 1 warehouse-style Logistics Center over a 200 hours planning horizon, with 1 replication in the run.

