tions at Plant A are centered on a coating step that takes exactly 27.0 hours per cycle and converts resin, frame_structure, memory_chip, and aluminum_sheet into solar_panel output. The site manages aluminum_sheet, frame_structure, memory_chip, solar_panel, and resin, and it also uses a Quality Scanner resource with capacity for 4 units at a time; that scanner runs with a fixed 7.764 hour service time and an operating cost of $8.733 per hour. On the demand side, Schneider Electric places orders for solar_panel every 9.0 hours, with each order ranging from 34312.0 units to 1224657.0 units. Customer deliveries to Schneider Electric take exactly 10.0 hours, the selling price is $2523.542 per unit, payment comes 85.0 hours later, and if stock is unavailable the sale is lost. ABB Industrial behaves differently: orders arrive every 42.0 hours, demand is normally centered on 3036291.0 units with variability of 59975.0 units, delivery takes between 4.0 hours and 13.0 hours, and solar_panel is sold to this customer at $907.555 per unit. In ABB’s case, available units ship immediately and any remaining demand is backordered, with payment collected between 17.0 hours and 34.0 hours later. Structurally, the node set includes CarbonSupply Co., FastChip Corp., PrecisionParts, Plant A, and the Distribution Center, with raw materials flowing from each supplier into Plant A and finished solar_panel units moving from Plant A to the Distribution Center. The model runs in hours over a 365 hour horizon with 10 replications.

