This network makes CPU units using carbon_fiber and resin, with sourcing split across six suppliers feeding the Production Center and finished CPUs then moving on to the Fulfillment Hub. The build structure is layered: each motor_assembly uses 5 units of carbon_fiber, each display_panel uses 2 units of resin, and each frame_structure consumes 5 units of carbon_fiber plus 3 units of resin. The final CPU then pulls together 3 units of display_panel, 3 units of resin, 4 units of frame_structure, 3 units of motor_assembly, and 1 unit of carbon_fiber. Carbon_fiber is bought on an as-needed basis and carries a holding cost of $2.833 per unit with stock reviewed every 30 hours. Resin follows a very different rhythm, arriving in fixed lots of 84716.0 units on a regular schedule, with deliveries showing up somewhere between 17.0 hours and 18.0 hours apart; its holding cost is $6.584 per unit, its shortage cost is $40.985 per unit, and it is reviewed every 20 hours. On the supply side, MegaSupply sends carbon_fiber to the Production Center with a reliable 19.0-hour lead time at $136.245 per unit, and payment is due between 30.0 days and 38.0 days after receipt. ChipCo is another carbon_fiber source, averaging 11.0 hours of lead time, charging $109.206 per unit, with payment fixed at 43.0 days. GlobalComponents is the fastest carbon_fiber option at an average of 6.0 hours, costs $85.638 per unit, and is paid in 52.0 to 64.0 days; its shipments to the plant take between 7.0 hours and 9.0 hours to transfer. For resin, Process Go. supplies at an average lead time of 13.0 hours, charges $450.832 per unit, offers 29.0- to 72.0-day payment terms, and can supply up to 602561 units per delivery. Memory Star provides resin with an average lead time of 27.0 hours at $3.768 per unit, on 57.0-day terms, and its transfer into the Production Center takes exactly 7.0 hours. ElectroParts also feeds resin into the site, with delivery ranging from 14.0 hours to 23.0 hours, a unit cost of $146.84, payment between 48.0 days and 84.0 days, and a maximum order size of 892167 units. Inside the operation, the Production Center manages display_panel, frame_structure, CPU, resin, carbon_fiber, and motor_assembly inventory. It starts with 2594334 units of motor_assembly, 1079825 units of display_panel, 1745605 units of frame_structure, and 1425855 units of CPU stock on hand. Those in-process items are monitored tightly: motor_assembly has a holding cost of $9.135 per unit, a shortage cost of $13.884 per unit, and a 1-hour review cycle; display_panel runs at $1.188 per unit to hold, $0.299 per unit for shortages, and is also reviewed every 1 hour; frame_structure carries $3.637 per unit in holding cost, $30.971 per unit in shortage cost, and the same 1-hour review interval. The fabrication step at the Production Center converts display_panel, resin, frame_structure, motor_assembly, and carbon_fiber into CPU units, with each run taking between 6.0 hours and 21.0 hours and requiring the Quality Scanner. That Quality Scanner has capacity for 4 units, works with an average service time of 9.549 hours, and costs $8.152 per hour to operate. There is also a Packaging Machine with capacity for 2 units, an average service time of 4.06 hours, and an operating cost of $10.756 per hour; it is not perfectly reliable, staying up for an average of 176.018 hours before failing and then being down for about 42.106 hours. Demand comes from Infineon Technologies, which places CPU orders every 43.0 hours, typically for 3330171.0 units with variability of about 358483.0 units. Customer delivery expectations run between 13.0 hours and 26.0 hours, and the business uses a partial backorder approach, meaning available units ship right away and the remaining balance waits until stock is replenished. CPUs sell at $4878.215 per unit, and customer payment is received 73.0 days later. The modeled setup runs for 365 hours, with 10 replications in total.

