 per unit and the same 1 week review cycle. Finished inventory begins at 4605368 units of smartphone and 4303135 units of laptop. Smartphone stock has a holding cost of $8.96 per unit, a shortage cost of $37.253 per unit, and is reviewed every 1 week. Manufacturing is centered at the Pilot Line, which manages circuit_board, gold_wire, silicon_wafer, smartphone, and motor_assembly for one production flow. There, an assembly step converts silicon_wafer, circuit_board, motor_assembly, and gold_wire into smartphone in 6.0 to 8.0 weeks per cycle, using the Laser Cutter. That Laser Cutter has capacity for 4 units, works with a service time between 0.929 weeks and 9.154 weeks, and batching is turned on with a batch size of 55 units and a maximum wait of 16.669 weeks. Running it costs $16.901 per week. The same Pilot Line also handles laptop, gold_wire, and lens_assembly in a separate welding flow, turning gold_wire and lens_assembly into laptop in 15.0 to 27.0 weeks per cycle. Storage downstream is handled by the Regional Depot warehouse. In the network layout, GlobalComponents and SiliconWorks feed Pilot Line and Regional Depot as the operating locations. Silicon_wafer moves from GlobalComponents to Pilot Line in exactly 1.0 week, while gold_wire also moves from SiliconWorks to Pilot Line. Both smartphone and laptop are then shipped from Pilot Line to Regional Depot. On the demand side, Ross Associates buys smartphone with orders arriving on an exponential pattern averaging 54.0 weeks, and each order is around 2349304.0 units with variability of 63312.0 units. Customer lead time is exactly 28.0 weeks. If stock is short, available units are shipped and the remainder is lost. The smartphone sells to this account at $3309.974 per unit, and payment comes 71.0 weeks later. Sony Electronics purchases laptop every 20.0 weeks flat, in fixed quantities of 1397328.0 units, with delivery promised in 14.0 weeks. If inventory is unavailable, the sale is lost entirely. The selling price there is $2364.563 per unit, and payment arrives between 22.0 weeks and 59.0 weeks. Infineon Technologies also buys laptop, placing orders somewhere between 9.0 weeks and 42.0 weeks apart, with demand centered on 1174634.0 units and varying by 294128.0 units. Their requested fulfillment window runs from 13.0 weeks to 18.0 weeks, and the policy is partial shipment with the balance lost. That business is priced at $4226.218 per unit with payment due in 25.0 weeks. The model runs in weeks over a 100 week planning horizon, with 10 replications.

