.0 days, and it can deliver up to 2331 units per order. Memory Star offers gold_wire as well, with lead time varying between 6.0 days and 10.0 days, priced at $396.064 per unit and paid 88.0 days after receipt. PolymerSource is another gold_wire source, taking 9.0 days flat at $431.459 per unit with net 56.0 days, and capacity capped at 492799 units. Carbon_fiber comes from CarbonSupply Co. at $117.442 per unit with lead times between 9.0 days and 26.0 days and payment due after 19.0 days; Process Go. supplies the same material at $253.201 per unit, usually arriving in 7.0 to 13.0 days, with 83.0-day payment terms and a limit of 285467 units per delivery; GlobalComponents sells carbon_fiber at $103.141 per unit with a 10.0- to 14.0-day lead time, 44.0-day payment, and capacity up to 922316 units; MegaSupply is the low-price source at $49.741 per unit, delivering in exactly 12.0 days with payment due in 59.0 days. Material movement into the plant is not identical by source. CarbonSupply Co. takes another 7.0 days to get carbon_fiber to the Pilot Line, PrecisionParts needs 6.0 to 8.0 days to move gold_wire in, Process Go. adds a 10.0-day transfer, Memory Star runs anywhere from 1.0 day to 10.0 days, GlobalComponents ships in 2.0 to 6.0 days, and MegaSupply adds 9.0 days; ChipCo and PolymerSource feed the Pilot Line without any stated transfer delay. Inside the Pilot Line, the camera flow is handled through a cutting step that uses gold_wire, battery_pack, and display_panel as inputs and produces camera, with each run taking 13.0 days. That step requires the Packaging Machine, which has capacity for 4 units, works with an exponential service time averaging 7.39 days, and incurs an operating cost of $11.727 per day. The machine is not perfectly reliable: it stays up for 290.055 days at a time and, when it fails, downtime follows an exponential pattern averaging 38.229 days. The same Pilot Line also runs a polishing step for router, consuming frame_structure, carbon_fiber, and gold_wire to produce router, again on a fixed 13.0-day cycle, but without any named resource requirement. Both camera and router then move from the Pilot Line to the Distribution Center. On the demand side, TechCorp buys camera, placing orders every 17.0 to 18.0 days for a constant 2107451.0 units. Customer delivery for those orders is promised in 11.0 days, unmet demand is backordered, the selling price is $4323.007 per unit, and payment is collected 13.0 to 53.0 days later. Lenovo Group purchases router on a steadier schedule, every 37.0 days, with demand fixed at 580804.0 units per order. Router shipments take 17.0 to 26.0 days to reach the customer, the business ships what is available and loses the remainder, the selling price is $1279.463 per unit, and Lenovo pays 70.0 days after receipt. The modeled network explicitly links 8 suppliers—ChipCo, CarbonSupply Co., PrecisionParts, Process Go., Memory Star, GlobalComponents, PolymerSource, and MegaSupply—to 2 facilities, the Pilot Line and the Distribution Center. The run itself covers 500 days, with results collected after a 30-day warm-up period.

